#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093234} :activity/id #uuid "2f440ce7-75c4-5ffe-a946-16960f17a55a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050612 :topic/id #uuid "58cd99c7-ea79-5204-8fe5-0ad9cfae548a" :topic/math-academy-id 918} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093481 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ be two (dependent) random variables. We say that $\\mathbf{X}$ is a **bivariate normal vector** with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ if the joint probability density function of $\\mathbf{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, \n\n- $\\sigma^2_1 = \\textrm{Var}[X_1]$ and $\\sigma^2_2 = \\textrm{Var}[X_2]$ are the variances of $X_1$ and $X_2,$\n\n- $\\sigma_{12} = \\sigma_{21} = \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2]$ is the covariance of $X_1$ and $X_2$,\n\n- $\\rho$ is the linear correlation coefficient of $X_1$ and $X_2,$ and\n\n- $|\\Sigma|$ denotes the determinant of $\\Sigma,$ the covariance matrix.\n\nA graph of a typical density function of a bivariate normal vector is depicted below. \n\n![](images/d1/d195358d6dc5b7b8a1080d53c774ee4289ce0f97c17de3bf45a52e86c6b20e29.png)\n\n \n\nThis surface is often referred to as having a \"bell shape.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfec4e77-fdaf-5a78-9be4-8fdc89abc5bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6997 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e60ad5d9-9bd3-5e95-8514-6ca6117c0c14" :step/math-academy-id 42251 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092330 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091934 :question/id #uuid "48d7b03b-c991-574a-bf15-8765ca43724b" :question/math-academy-id "e-11113"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece25ba-5df0-5b73-8cc5-7788b17fdcc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050280}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549067}] :knowledge-point/title "The Joint PDF of a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "af6e2582-70c7-596f-8bcd-b192815cf772" :step/math-academy-id 42252 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092331 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091935 :question/id #uuid "90ff4bf9-f8d3-5179-9d02-0e0a46a3cc98" :question/math-academy-id "e-11114"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c646fe15-1709-599e-b5d5-308b1dcb3271" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049812}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549056}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Bivariate Normal Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "93c0e956-bdbc-5097-ae5f-36a3027a1849" :step/math-academy-id 42253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092332 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091936 :question/id #uuid "f9e6c722-6017-57b9-9de6-c83c2f14c8c4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11115"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e2025f8-a9cf-5e30-bb03-4909aefdd7e2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549044}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Combinations of Elements From a Multivariate Normal Random Variable"} :step/id #uuid "eb1f1c89-4a73-5654-9bf4-8fbb33f576a8" :step/math-academy-id 42254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093482 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we compared the PDF of $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nand the joint PDF of a bivariate normal distribution $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2]^T$ with mean vector $\\boldsymbol\\mu$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}2}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can generalize this for an arbitrary number of variables ${\\color{red}p},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}p}/2} {\\color{blue}|\\Sigma|}^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,{\\color{blue}\\Sigma}^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right)\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_p)$ is called the **multivariate normal** density function. A random vector $\\mathbf X = [X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_p]^T$ with this joint density function is said to follow a **multivariate normal distribution**.\n\nSimilar to the bivariate case, we can consider linear combinations of coordinates of multivariate normal random vectors: \n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} =  [X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k]^T$ is a multivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ then the random variable $Y,$ defined as \n\n$$\nY= \\mathbf c^T \\mathbf X = \\sum_{i=1}^k c_i X_i\n$$\n\n is a normally distributed random variable, where $\\mathbf c$ is any $k\\times 1$ column vector, and*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & ={c}^{T}\\mu , \\\\ Var[Y] & ={c}^{T}Σc.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the variance is a quadratic form." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2ce75d0-2db0-5254-880f-d1042f119f29" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6998 :tutorial/title "The Multivariate Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "f937d23e-c522-5623-bb89-f42667039185" :step/math-academy-id 42260 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093483 :tutorial/content "Recall that a normally distributed random variable $X\\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}} e^{-\\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma} \\right)^2}.\n$$\n\nThis probability density function can be written as \n\n$$\nf(x) = (2\\pi)^{-{\\color{red}1}/2 } ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1/2} \\: \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\frac{1}{2} (x-\\mu) ({\\color{blue}\\sigma^2})^{-1} (x-\\mu) \\right).\n$$\n\nLet's compare this to the density function of a bivariate normal vector. First, we rewrite the joint density $f(x_1,x_2)$ of the bivariate normal distribution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{|Σ|}}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-1}|Σ{|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu )) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-2/2}{|Σ|}^{-1/2}\\,exp(-\\frac{1}{2}(X-\\mu )^{T}\\,{Σ}^{-1}\\,(X-\\mu ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy comparing the expressions for $f(x)$ and $f(x_1,x_2),$ we see that the *bivariate* normal distribution can be considered as a generalization of the *normal distribution* from the ${\\color{red}1}$-dimensional case (when we have only one variable, $x$) to the ${\\color{red}2}$-dimensional case (when we have two variables, $x_1$ and $x_2$).\n\nThis analogy will be particularly helpful when extending the bivariate normal distribution to more than two random variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "198d2a80-9efc-509c-8852-d560b1203524" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7016 :tutorial/title "Normal Distribution vs. Bivariate Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "56eb632e-0a3e-5c4c-a62a-0984c2a4a72a" :step/math-academy-id 42318 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093484 :tutorial/content "A linear combination of coordinates of a bivariate normal random vector generates a (one-dimensional) normal distribution:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma,$ given by*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*then the random variable $Y,$ defined as *\n\n$$\nY= aX_1 + bX_2\n$$\n\n*is a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b.$ Furthermore, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "62a410d8-f059-5273-ba3a-15646c153a7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7017 :tutorial/title "Linear Combinations of Normal Random Variables From Bivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "35262d1e-3ec3-5c70-a2dc-cca3fc1c3e76" :step/math-academy-id 42319 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093485 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a bivariate normal vector $\\mathbf{X} = [X_1, X_2]^T$ with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x_1, x_2) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{|\\Sigma|}} \\mathrm{exp}\\left( -\\dfrac 12(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere the mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ and covariance matrix $\\Sigma$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =[\\begin{aligned}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} \\\\ \\rho {\\sigma }_{1}{\\sigma }_{2} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMost of the parameters of the distribution can be interpreted geometrically on a corresponding graph of the probability density function:\n\n- The mean vector $\\boldsymbol{\\mu}$ gives the $xy$-coordinates of the peak.\n\n- The correlation coefficient $\\rho$ defines how much the bell is squeezed towards the plane $y=x{:}$ \n\n- when $\\rho \\to 0,$ the horizontal cross-sections are almost circular, while\n\n- when $\\rho \\to 1,$ the shape becomes more and more squeezed towards the plane $y=x.$\n\n![](images/5b/5bd69db417134cccedd5af45cb56fb44f27925ba5a8470f3b5622f0856ed855d.png)\n\n \n\n- The variances $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ represent how much the curve is spread out along the principal axes $x_1=\\mu_1$ and $x_2=\\mu_2{:}$\n\n- when $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are small, the bell shape is more \"pointy,\" while\n\n- for larger $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2,$ the bell shape is more \"flat.\"\n\nThe level curves of the surface have equations of the form\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu)^T \\,\\Sigma^{-1} \\,(\\mathbf X - \\boldsymbol \\mu) = \\textrm{const},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhich represent ellipses centered at $(\\mu_1,\\mu_2).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6b5643b7ab0568faa0b6e16b55a11482000b09d1b5862158d4ac9649281bb7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0f898055-8522-58fc-8460-b4e13ed3afb7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7018 :tutorial/title "Interpretation of Parameters in a Bivariate Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "f16fc1c1-fc47-5b78-88b4-8c18145f6d33" :step/math-academy-id 42320 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093235}}] :activity/title "The Bivariate Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092422}]}
